# MUV Construction: How the Vehicle Is Built

The MUV is constructed primarily from perforated square steel tubing, angle iron, plywood, and common fasteners. The structure is bolted together rather than welded, making it possible to modify, disassemble, and reconfigure sections as the vehicle evolves.

The construction is relatively straightforward, but the way the components fit together is important. The chassis provides the foundation, while the nested tubing, removable assemblies, and accessible compartments make the vehicle serviceable and adaptable.

This document describes the current construction of the prototype. It is an explanation of what I built, rather than a dimensioned fabrication plan.

## 1. The Main Chassis

The primary chassis structure uses 1½-inch perforated square steel tubing.

Each side begins with a single, unaltered four-foot length of square tubing. These two pieces form the main side members of the chassis.

A second piece of the same larger square tubing is cut and bent at four locations to create a dropped profile. This lower support follows the general shape needed to support the side structure and provide a lower mounting area. It is bolted to the unaltered four-foot member.

The result is two side assemblies, each consisting of a straight upper member and a formed lower support.

The left and right side assemblies are connected by four short sections of the same larger square tubing. These are the frame stabilizers, or crossmembers. Two are positioned beneath the side assemblies, while the other two connect the front structure.

Together, these pieces establish the basic chassis width and provide the structural openings needed for the battery, front assembly, and other components.

## 2. Battery Platform

The battery occupies the open area between the two main side assemblies.

A wooden panel is placed on top of the lower frame supports to create a platform for the 48V battery. The four frame stabilizers help define the space in which the battery sits.

This arrangement keeps the battery low within the chassis while allowing it to be accessed through the surrounding structure.

## 3. Rear Drive Assembly

The rear drive assembly uses the smaller, 1¼-inch perforated square tubing.

The wheel mounting arrangement consists of two individual pieces of this smaller tubing, each approximately six to eight inches long. These short pieces are attached to the rear-wheel assembly.

They slide into the larger square tubing incorporated into the lower side-frame structure.

This is an important feature of the construction. The smaller pieces are not long continuous rails. They are individual slide-in members that connect the rear-wheel assembly to the chassis.

The arrangement allows the rear wheel assembly to be removed without dismantling the main chassis. I have taken it apart and reassembled it several times, and it has proven to be a very practical solution.

It is one of the features I am particularly pleased with because it makes routine service and future changes much easier.

## 4. Front Structure and Glove Box

The front portion of the chassis uses two altered pieces of the smaller, 1¼-inch square tubing.

These pieces insert into the larger side-frame members and extend forward to establish the structure in front of the rider. They form the basic mounting arrangement for the glove box.

The front structure is connected using the remaining two frame stabilizers. These crossmembers add rigidity to the chassis and provide the mounting points for the front forks.

The forks are secured to the front crossmembers using two three-inch U-bolts. Shims can be used where needed to improve the fit.

The front wheel is smaller than the conventional wheel size normally associated with the forks. The vehicle's geometry does not provide enough room for the expected wheel size, so a smaller wheel was used. In actual riding, this has not negatively affected the experience.

## 5. Seat and Upper Bracing

The seat consists of a wooden base with a foam-covered upper surface.

Two pieces of perforated angle iron run along the upper portion of the chassis. These pieces provide additional bracing and create a solid mounting structure for the seat.

The seat is positioned between the angle-iron members. In addition to supporting the rider, it serves as a cover for the battery area.

## 6. Motor Controller and Electrical Components

The motor controller is located in the base of the chassis, beneath the glove box.

Its electrical connections face rearward, toward the battery. This orientation keeps the connections directed toward the battery location.

The battery, controller, and associated wiring are concealed behind the side panels.

## 7. Side Panels

The side panels are made from ¼-inch luan plywood.

They serve two purposes: they give the vehicle a more finished appearance and provide protection for the battery, controller, and wiring.

They also help shield these components from dirt, moisture, and debris kicked up while riding.

The panels are part of the practical enclosure of the vehicle, rather than simply decorative bodywork.

## 8. Glove Box Floor and Storage

The glove box has a two-piece floor made from ¼-inch plywood. The two sections are connected using two hinges.

The arrangement provides several ways to access the space below.

The driver can remove the floor, raise the front section, and slide the other section forward. This exposes the battery charger, charging plug, and tool kit.

The tool kit includes wrenches, Allen keys, and an air pump.

There is also an unexpected secondary use for this arrangement. When the front section is raised and the two pieces are moved forward, they create 
a slot that can hold a Bluetooth speaker. The speaker can then be used with a media player while riding.

It is a small example of how practical features have emerged through using the vehicle rather than simply designing it on paper.

## 9. KICKSTAND

The kickstand is made from a truncated triangular arrangement of threaded rod that fits into holes in the chassis near the front of the vehicle.

A scrap piece of square tubing is bolted to one side of the assembly. This acts as a stop to prevent the kickstand from rotating too far forward and 
allowing the vehicle to topple.

It is a simple solution made from available materials, and it has worked well for its intended purpose.

